WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.105
Responses should include only the data
needed by the caller and use case.

2
00:00:04.185 --> 00:00:06.963
Excessive data exposure often comes from
convenience:

3
00:00:07.043 --> 00:00:10.825
a database record is already available, so
the handler returns it directly.

4
00:00:10.905 --> 00:00:13.742
That shortcut can expose password hashes,
reset tokens,

5
00:00:13.822 --> 00:00:17.486
internal flags, administrative notes,
billing metadata, secret names,

6
00:00:17.566 --> 00:00:19.635
or fields intended only for another role.

7
00:00:19.715 --> 00:00:24.029
Response models, serializers, or explicit
object construction make output shaping a

8
00:00:24.109 --> 00:00:25.705
deliberate security boundary.

9
00:00:25.785 --> 00:00:29.803
Template rendering and HTML responses
require context-aware output handling.

10
00:00:29.883 --> 00:00:33.666
Data that is safe as plain text may not be
safe inside an HTML attribute, a URL,

11
00:00:33.746 --> 00:00:35.932
a script context, or rendered rich
content.

12
00:00:36.012 --> 00:00:39.795
Express applications may serve APIs,
server-rendered pages, admin tools,

13
00:00:39.875 --> 00:00:42.002
and email previews from the same codebase,

14
00:00:42.082 --> 00:00:44.387
so output rules should match the
destination.

15
00:00:44.467 --> 00:00:48.604
Avoid assuming that a value is safe simply
because it has already been validated for

16
00:00:48.684 --> 00:00:50.044
storage or business logic.

17
00:00:50.124 --> 00:00:54.379
Errors should help clients recover and
help engineers investigate without exposing

18
00:00:54.459 --> 00:00:55.050
internals.

19
00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:59.089
Production responses should avoid stack
traces, internal paths, SQL details,

20
00:00:59.169 --> 00:01:02.952
tokens, secret names, dependency
internals, and implementation details.

21
00:01:03.032 --> 00:01:07.228
Internal logs can preserve diagnostic
context with access controls and redaction,

22
00:01:07.308 --> 00:01:10.322
while client responses stay safe,
consistent, and limited.

23
00:01:10.402 --> 00:01:12.057
A mature error path is not silent;

24
00:01:12.137 --> 00:01:15.860
it separates what operators need to know
from what callers should receive.
